;var SalesTaxCity=surtaxBase*CityRate;var SalesTaxSpec=surtaxBase*SpecRate;var TotalSalesTax=SalesTaxState+SalesTaxCountry+SalesTaxCity+SalesTaxSpec;return TotalSalesTax});//# sourceURL=customJs.js
    TXT,
    'article' => [
        'intro' => <<<'TXT'
        Florida sales tax takes Purchase amount and Purchase location ZIP Code and returns Sales tax. There is no hidden constant here: change sales tax and purchase amount moves with it.
        TXT,
        'sections' => [
            ['title' => 'How the Florida sales tax calculator works', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>The Florida sales tax calculator applies one relationship to 2 numbers and reports Sales tax.</p><p>In the form&#039;s own words, Sales tax = get_sales_tax(Purchase amount, Purchase location ZIP Code).</p><p>The result panel carries Sales tax, each in a box of its own rather than buried in a sentence.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'What to enter, and what comes back', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>Fill in 2 boxes and the page has everything it needs.</p><ul><li><strong>Purchase amount</strong> — a plain number, no unit</li><li><strong>Purchase location ZIP Code</strong> — a plain number, no unit</li></ul><p>The answer comes back as Sales tax. The page writes that box; there is nothing to submit or save before you can read it.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'Reading the answer', 'html' => '<p>None of the boxes on this page carries a unit chip: Sales tax takes a plain number and the answer inherits whatever scale the inputs were measured in.</p>'],
            ['title' => 'The formula, and what turns it round', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>Below is the arithmetic in full. Every symbol on the right of an equals sign is one of the boxes on the form.</p><ul><li><strong>Sales tax</strong> = <code>get_sales_tax(Purchase amount, Purchase location ZIP Code)</code></li></ul>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'Checking an answer by hand', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>There is one relationship on this page, so any answer can be checked by putting it back into the box that owns it and seeing whether the remaining ones agree.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'Where the numbers usually go wrong', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <ul><li><strong>The answer typed back in.</strong> A result box can be typed into, and doing so changes the question rather than the arithmetic: with Sales tax is a result filled in, the page solves for a different variable instead. Check which box is blank before reading the panel.</li><li><strong>Purchase amount left as a guess.</strong> A figure entered here because the box had to have something in it is the single most common way a correct formula produces a wrong answer. If Purchase amount is unknown, solve for it instead of estimating it — leave it blank.</li><li><strong>Purchase location ZIP Code left as a guess.</strong> A figure entered here because the box had to have something in it is the single most common way a correct formula produces a wrong answer. If Purchase location ZIP Code is unknown, solve for it instead of estimating it — leave it blank.</li><li><strong>Measurements mixed across scales.</strong> Entering a rounded value is fine; rounding twice is not. Keep full precision in Purchase amount and let the panel round the answer once.</li></ul>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'What each box means', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>The boxes on the Florida sales tax form, one at a time: what each one wants, how it is measured, and what the page does if you leave it empty.</p><ul><li><strong>Purchase amount</strong> takes a plain number with no unit attached. The page reads it when it works out Sales tax. Fill it from a measurement rather than a memory — Purchase amount is the kind of value that gets estimated once and then copied from page to page for years.</li><li><strong>Purchase location ZIP Code</strong> takes a plain number with no unit attached. The page reads it when it works out Sales tax. Fill it from a measurement rather than a memory — Purchase location ZIP Code is the kind of value that gets estimated once and then copied from page to page for years.</li></ul><p>Every one of those boxes is optional in a sense: fill all but one and the page solves for the one you left. It is only when two are blank that the page has nothing to choose between.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'What each result tells you', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>Each box below is a different question about the same calculation. Read them in this order:</p><ul><li><strong>Sales tax</strong> is a plain number. It is computed from Purchase amount and Purchase location ZIP Code, so it changes the moment any of them does. Read it as an order of magnitude first and a decimal second: a result that is out by a factor of ten usually means a unit or a decimal point, not a formula that has stopped working. It is not stored, logged or carried to the next page: change a box and you get a new number, close the tab and it is gone.</li></ul>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'Getting an answer, step by step', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
